1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,822
Seven in the evening in Suryagaon.

2
00:00:02,822 --> 00:00:04,619
The lights go out again.

3
00:00:04,919 --> 00:00:07,211
Kittu asks his sister one question.

4
00:00:07,211 --> 00:00:09,902
Where does our electricity even come from?

5
00:00:10,452 --> 00:00:11,352
Meet Meera.

6
00:00:11,323 --> 00:00:14,947
She has just started engineering,
in electrical engineering.

7
00:00:15,297 --> 00:00:17,034
Her village has a problem.

8
00:00:17,034 --> 00:00:20,707
The power goes off most evenings,
and the farm pumps stop.

9
00:00:21,057 --> 00:00:25,060
Shankar anna, a retired lineman,
knows every pole in the village.

10
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:29,434
And Farida, the panchayat president,
wants to know what it would cost to

11
00:00:29,434 --> 00:00:30,334
fix.

12
00:00:30,528 --> 00:00:32,869
So what is electrical engineering?

13
00:00:33,169 --> 00:00:37,301
It is the branch of engineering that
makes electricity, moves it, and puts

14
00:00:37,301 --> 00:00:38,201
it to work.

15
00:00:38,507 --> 00:00:43,486
Electrical engineers build power
stations, wires, motors, and the circuits

16
00:00:43,486 --> 00:00:45,070
inside every machine.

17
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:49,426
Let's follow the power, from the
power station to Meera's switch.

18
00:00:49,776 --> 00:00:52,193
A power station spins a generator.

19
00:00:52,193 --> 00:00:55,677
It makes electricity at
about eleven thousand volts.

20
00:00:56,027 --> 00:01:00,321
A step up transformer raises it to
four hundred thousand volts, for the

21
00:01:00,321 --> 00:01:01,299
long journey.

22
00:01:01,649 --> 00:01:05,337
Tall towers carry it hundreds
of kilometres across the state.

23
00:01:05,687 --> 00:01:10,364
Near the town, a substation steps it
down to thirty three thousand, then

24
00:01:10,364 --> 00:01:11,909
eleven thousand volts.

25
00:01:12,259 --> 00:01:16,354
On the village pole, a small transformer
brings it down to two hundred

26
00:01:16,354 --> 00:01:17,537
and thirty volts.

27
00:01:17,887 --> 00:01:21,752
It passes the meter, reaches the
switchboard, and the bulb glows.

28
00:01:22,402 --> 00:01:24,817
But why raise the voltage so high?

29
00:01:24,817 --> 00:01:25,754
Watch this.

30
00:01:26,104 --> 00:01:30,551
Send one hundred kilowatts down a
line with one ohm of resistance.

31
00:01:30,901 --> 00:01:34,021
At one thousand volts, a
hundred amps must flow.

32
00:01:34,021 --> 00:01:36,753
The wire heats up and
wastes ten kilowatts.

33
00:01:37,103 --> 00:01:40,125
At ten thousand volts, only ten amps flow.

34
00:01:40,125 --> 00:01:42,942
Now the waste is just one hundred watts.

35
00:01:43,292 --> 00:01:46,372
Ten times the voltage, one
hundredth of the waste.

36
00:01:46,372 --> 00:01:48,799
The loss grows with the current squared.

37
00:01:49,449 --> 00:01:53,776
Raise the voltage, lower the current,
and the wires waste far less.

38
00:01:54,476 --> 00:01:57,467
At home, the power is
AC, alternating current.

39
00:01:57,817 --> 00:02:01,615
The voltage swings up and
down, fifty times every second.

40
00:02:01,615 --> 00:02:03,105
That is fifty hertz.

41
00:02:03,454 --> 00:02:07,380
We call it two hundred and thirty
volts, but each swing peaks near three

42
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:08,826
hundred and twenty five.

43
00:02:09,476 --> 00:02:11,443
Where do we see this at work today?

44
00:02:11,793 --> 00:02:12,693
Make.

45
00:02:12,382 --> 00:02:16,862
A power station control room keeps
every generator turning in step.

46
00:02:17,212 --> 00:02:18,112
Move.

47
00:02:17,798 --> 00:02:22,641
Since twenty thirteen, all of India's
regional grids run as one, at fifty

48
00:02:22,641 --> 00:02:23,541
hertz.

49
00:02:23,629 --> 00:02:24,530
Step down.

50
00:02:24,530 --> 00:02:28,835
A big hospital has its own
transformer, and a backup generator.

51
00:02:29,185 --> 00:02:30,085
Use.

52
00:02:29,783 --> 00:02:33,450
A metro railway runs every
train on electricity.

53
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:36,045
So is electricity simply good?

54
00:02:36,045 --> 00:02:37,904
It depends how we treat it.

55
00:02:38,254 --> 00:02:39,349
The good side.

56
00:02:39,349 --> 00:02:43,980
Light to study, water for fields,
and machines that save hours of work.

57
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:45,431
The hard side.

58
00:02:45,431 --> 00:02:48,743
Wires waste some power,
and a live wire can kill.

59
00:02:49,093 --> 00:02:52,601
Kittu wants to climb the
pole to see the transformer.

60
00:02:52,601 --> 00:02:54,145
Shankar anna says no.

61
00:02:54,495 --> 00:02:58,910
Only a trained lineman works on a line,
after it is switched off and earthed.

62
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:00,727
History helps too.

63
00:03:00,727 --> 00:03:06,385
In nineteen oh two, a hydro power station
at Shivanasamudra falls, in Karnataka,

64
00:03:06,385 --> 00:03:09,109
sent power to the Kolar Gold Fields.

65
00:03:09,459 --> 00:03:10,384
The lesson?

66
00:03:10,384 --> 00:03:13,678
Use electricity fully,
but respect it every time.

67
00:03:14,328 --> 00:03:15,383
Quick check.

68
00:03:15,383 --> 00:03:19,464
Why do power lines carry
electricity at very high voltage?

69
00:03:21,364 --> 00:03:22,615
To cut the loss!

70
00:03:22,615 --> 00:03:27,253
Higher voltage means less current,
and far less heat wasted in the wires.

71
00:03:27,853 --> 00:03:28,862
So, remember.

72
00:03:29,162 --> 00:03:33,560
Electrical engineering makes electricity,
moves it, and puts it to work.

73
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:37,450
Power is stepped up for the journey,
and stepped down for your home.

74
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:41,885
At home it is AC, two hundred
and thirty volts, at fifty hertz.

75
00:03:42,485 --> 00:03:46,489
Next time, we'll meet charge,
current, voltage, power and energy.
